  {
    "index": 3,
    "hash": "aEriPT9rUfEA3OrQGvftxKKft4c=",
    "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:12.249Z",
    "action": {
      "type": "SET_CONTENT",
      "input": {
        "content": "Every operation travels with its context:\n\n```ts\ntype OperationWithContext = {\n  operation: Operation;\n  context: {\n    documentId: string;\n    documentType: string;\n    scope: string;\n    branch: string;\n    resultingState?: string;\n    ordinal: number; // global ordering across ALL operations\n  };\n};\n```\n\nTwo of those fields are load-bearing rather than informational.\n\n**`resultingState`** is what `IDocumentView` applies. The architecture is explicit that the view *requires* it and **does not rebuild from operations** \u2014 so a producer that omits it does not merely slow the read model down, it leaves the snapshot unable to advance. This is the price of a read model that never replays: the writer must hand it the state.\n\n**`ordinal`** is a global sequence across every operation in the reactor, assigned by the operation index. It is what sync cursors track, because a cursor over a flattened multi-document stream cannot use per-scope `index` values.\n\nThe distinction matters when writing a processor or a custom read model: `operation.index` is a position inside one scope, `context.ordinal` is a position in the reactor's whole history.\n\n## Provenance\n- `packages/reactor/docs/ARCHITECTURE.md` \u2014 Core Concepts \u203a OperationWithContext; Components \u203a DocumentView\n\n_Extracted from the Powerhouse repository at **v6.2.2-dev.74**._",
        "updatedAt": "2026-09-03T07:18:12.249Z"
      },
      "timestampUtcMs": "2026-09-03T07:18:12.249Z"
    }
  },
  {
